WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.320
We in Terrebonne, again, it goes back.

2
00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:11.780
There was a number of drainage districts formed, and there were a number of canals dug not only for logging purposes, but primarily for drainage purposes.

3
00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:20.300
There was one what they call the prairie land district drainage district which was between the Coteau Ridge, Bayou Blue Ridge, and Houma.

4
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:26.400
They dug a canal from Bayou Terrebonne to Bayou Coteau to Bayou Blue for drainage purposes.

5
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:30.640
That's where US 90 ended up by being built, right along the spoils from that canal.

6
00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:34.380
We had a great storm in 1856 that affected Terrebonne.

7
00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:39.740
There was a 1909 storm that affected Terrebonne which my father . . . grandfather,

8
00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:45.140
and a great uncle drowned in, but my father and grandfather survived the storm.

9
00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:48.520
Then there was a 1926 storm that would affect the Terrebonne.

10
00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:53.360
Then in later years, as far as I'm concerned, Betsy was a major storm.

11
00:00:53.360 --> 00:01:03.220
We've had a few others, and that's another book that I would like somebody to write one day about the storms that drastically affected Terrebonne Parish.

12
00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:11.460
Interesting, Houma has never flooded from a hurricane because of . . . I consider our buffer was the wetlands which are gone.

13
00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:20.020
Again I think we've lost over 400,000 acres of land between here and the Gulf of Mexico in Terrebonne Parish within my lifetime.

14
00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:22.880
That was our protection and that's gone.

15
00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:28.280
But again, in 1926 there was a hurricane.

16
00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:36.320
My father told me about this story that the hurricane created what we call Wonder Lake which was between Bayou Terrebonne and Bayou Pointe Aux Chenes.

17
00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:43.040
The storm with the tornados or et cetera picked up the marsh and put it in the middle of Bayou Terrebonne.

18
00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:48.560
They had to dredge Bayou Terrebonne out for navigation and drainage purposes.

19
00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:51.140
So, drainage was always a big deal.

20
00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:57.380
Most of our drainage until my lifetime, until my professional lifetime, was gravity drainage.

21
00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:01.980
We never had a drainage problem of influx of water.

22
00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.120
We always had a gravity drainage problem.

23
00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:11.140
Of course, our drainage system also was drastically affected by the leveeing of the Mississippi and the Atchafalaya River.

24
00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:18.360
We are between the basin of the Mississippi and the Atchafalaya and the Bayou Lafourche basin to the Gulf of Mexico.

25
00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:20.640
Love when the court talks about the basin.

26
00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.200
I said, "Well, first off when you talk about the basin,

27
00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:28.420
the basin that I live in starts in Minnesota and it comes all the way to the Gulf of Mexico,

28
00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:32.580
hoss! [laughing] Now I got a little basin where I live, but you want to talk about the basin?

29
00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:35.380
It's the big basin, and I'm part of the big basin!

30
00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:40.360
So don't do something in Minnesota and don't think about what the shit you're doing to me in Louisiana."

31
00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.620
Let me tell you they don't have a clue.

32
00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:43.740
Okay?

33
00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:49.620
I've been preaching that for fifty years, and it's like, I mean, I'd just as soon been talking to the chair.
